4. Root-Cause Diagnosis and Alert Rule
Primary root cause

Failing component: OrderingMicroservice and its downstream dependencies.

Root-cause explanation:

The checkout failures have two separate downstream causes:

Two checkout requests failed with HTTP 500 because calls to the SQL stored procedure sp_InsertCustomerOrder timed out. Both related SQL dependencies failed after approximately three seconds.
One checkout request failed with HTTP 504 because the payment authorization dependency did not respond before the gateway timeout.

The strongest repeated failure is the SQL dependency because it failed in two separate operations. The likely primary incident is therefore an OrderDb timeout affecting order persistence. The payment gateway timeout is an additional downstream failure affecting checkout processing.

Recommended Azure Monitor alert configuration

Alert name: OrderingMicroservice Checkout Failure Alert

Signal logic:

requests
| where timestamp >= ago(5m)
| where cloud_RoleName == "OrderingMicroservice"
| summarize
    TotalRequests=count(),
    FailedRequests=countif(success == false)
| extend FailureRate=100.0 * FailedRequests / TotalRequests
| where FailedRequests >= 2 or FailureRate >= 20


Threshold and frequency:

Evaluation frequency: Every 5 minutes
Lookback period: 5 minutes
Trigger when:
Failed requests are 2 or more, or
Failure rate is 20% or higher
Severity: Sev 2
Action group: Notify the OrderingMicroservice support team through email or Microsoft Teams.
Recommended SQL dependency alert
dependencies
| where timestamp >= ago(5m)
| where type == "SQL"
| where target contains "OrderDb"
| summarize
    TotalCalls=count(),
    FailedCalls=countif(success == false),
    AverageDurationMs=avg(duration)
| extend FailureRate=100.0 * FailedCalls / TotalCalls
| where FailedCalls >= 2 or FailureRate >= 20 or AverageDurationMs > 2000


Trigger the SQL alert when at least two calls fail, the failure rate reaches 20%, or the average dependency duration exceeds 2000 ms.

Conclusion

The telemetry identifies OrderingMicroservice as the affected service. Two checkout operations failed because OrderDb timed out during sp_InsertCustomerOrder, while another failed because payment authorization returned a 504 Gateway Timeout. Catalog and inventory dependencies remained healthy. The immediate investigation should focus on SQL stored-procedure performance, database blocking, connection-pool saturation, and payment-gateway timeout handling.

